---
import InnerPage from "../layouts/InnerPage.astro";

/* THE PILLAR PAGE FOR THE HEAD TERM, AND THE REASON IT DID NOT EXIST BEFORE.
 *
 * "multistream" is 3,600 US searches a month at KD 35 (DataForSEO, 16 August
 * 2026). Until this page, the site's answer to that query was the homepage,
 * whose title begins "Multistream, a different mix per platform" and whose H1 is
 * "One stream in. A different mix out of each."
 *
 * That H1 is the right hero line and the wrong landing for a generic query. A
 * reader typing "multistream" has not yet accepted the category, let alone the
 * subtlety that distinguishes this product inside it. The homepage was arguing
 * the differentiator to someone still asking what the thing is.
 *
 * So this page takes the category question and routes it, and the homepage keeps
 * selling the mix. It also gives /vs/* an internal-link parent, which the four
 * comparison pages did not have -- they were reachable from /comparison and the
 * footer and nothing else.
 *
 * WHAT THIS PAGE MUST NOT BECOME. A second homepage. It says what multistreaming
 * is, what the three architectures are, and where to go next. Every claim that
 * needs arguing is argued somewhere else and linked, because a pillar page that
 * re-argues its children is the same page published five times -- thin to a
 * crawler and boring to the reader who clicked through.
 *
 * The platform sections exist for "kick multistream" (170/mo, KD 5) and "youtube
 * multistream" (170/mo, KD 0). They are sections rather than pages on purpose:
 * separately they would be thin, and the honest answer to both is nearly the
 * same one.
 *
 * NO COMPETITOR CLAIM APPEARS HERE WITHOUT A SOURCE. The architecture table
 * names categories, not products, precisely so it does not need per-product
 * provenance; where a product is named, the claim traces to
 * docs/evidence/competitor-facts-2026-08-15.md and the /vs/ page carries it.
 */

const approaches = [
  {
    name: "Your PC does the work",
    how: "Your streaming PC uploads a separate copy of the stream for every destination. The encode can be shared across all of them or run independently per destination.",
    cost: "Upload multiplies by the number of destinations. CPU multiplies only if you re-encode per destination instead of sharing the encode OBS already made.",
    audio: "Whatever mix that output was given.",
    who: "obs-multi-rtmp, Aitum Multistream, SE.Live: plugins that live inside OBS.",
    href: "/vs/obs-multi-rtmp",
    hrefLabel: "How the plugins compare",
  },
  {
    name: "A cloud relay",
    how: "You send one stream to somebody else's server. They fan it out to the platforms for you.",
    cost: "One upload. A subscription once you pass a free tier's limits, and your stream passes through a third party.",
    audio: "restream.io duplicates the one stream it ingests to every endpoint, and Castr sends identical audio to all destinations (both checked 2026-08-15). Streamlabs does not describe per-destination audio either way, so nothing is claimed here.",
    who: "restream.io, Castr, Streamlabs Multistream.",
    href: "/vs/streamlabs",
    hrefLabel: "How the cloud relays compare",
  },
  {
    name: "A relay you run",
    how: "You send one stream to a server you own. It fans out, on your hardware, on your network.",
    cost: "One upload. No subscription. A box to run it on.",
    audio: "This is where polyemesis differs: a different mix per destination, summed server-side.",
    who: "polyemesis, datarhei Restreamer, MistServer.",
    href: "/vs/restreamer",
    hrefLabel: "How the self-hosted relays compare",
  },
];

const platforms = [
  {
    name: "Twitch",
    slug: "twitch",
    body:
      "Twitch is the destination that makes the audio question concrete. Its Enhanced Broadcasting can carry a second audio track alongside the live one, which is how the broadcast keeps a music bed while the archive gets a mix without it, from one upload and one encode.",
    caveat:
      "It needs a GPU on the machine polyemesis runs on: Twitch refuses the negotiation outright when the request reports none, which matters on the VPS this audience often uses.",
    href: "/twitch-vod-track",
    hrefLabel: "The Twitch VOD track, in detail",
  },
  {
    name: "YouTube",
    slug: "youtube",
    body:
      "YouTube takes one stereo pair, like every other live platform. Multistreaming to it is unremarkable. The interesting decision is which mix it gets, and that is the same decision for every destination in the fan-out.",
    caveat:
      "A second audio track is not offered here. Nothing in this project has measured YouTube accepting one, and the code refuses to try.",
    href: "/docs/audio-routing",
    hrefLabel: "How a mix is chosen",
  },
  {
    name: "Kick",
    slug: "kick",
    body:
      "Kick is an RTMP destination and behaves like one. polyemesis signs in to it for chat and metadata as well, so a Kick fan-out is not only video: the moderation timeline reads it alongside the others.",
    caveat:
      "Chat moderation covers five platforms: YouTube, Twitch, Kick, Facebook and Rumble. Facebook is read-and-moderate only; it is not in the send fan-out.",
    href: "/docs/platforms",
    hrefLabel: "What each platform can automate",
  },
];

const index = [
  { id: "the-three-architectures", label: "The three architectures" },
  { id: "where-polyemesis-sits", label: "Where polyemesis sits" },
  { id: "per-platform", label: "Per platform" },
];
---

<InnerPage
  longform
  title="Multistream to Twitch, YouTube and Kick from one upload"
  description="Three ways to multistream, what each costs in CPU and upload, and how to send a different audio mix to every destination from a single SRT ingest."
  path="/multistream"
  crumbs={[
    { label: "polyemesis", href: "/" },
    { label: "Multistream" },
  ]}
  heading="Multistreaming is one upload reaching several platforms. The question is what each one hears."
  lede="Going live to Twitch and YouTube at once is a solved problem with three well-known answers. Sending them different audio (the full mix to one, a music-free mix to another, your mic alone to a third) is where the three answers stop agreeing. This page covers both."
  index={index}
  band={{ heading: "A relay on hardware you control.", secondary: { label: "How the mix matrix works", href: "/docs/audio-routing" } }}
>
  <section id="the-three-architectures">
    <h2>The three architectures</h2>
    <p>
      Every multistreaming tool is one of these. Which one you want follows from
      what you are short of: upload bandwidth, CPU, money, or trust in a third
      party.
    </p>

    <div class="rows">
      {approaches.map((a) => (
        <article class="lf-split">
          <h3>{a.name}</h3>
          <div>
            <p>{a.how}</p>
            <dl class="lf-facts">
              <dt>Costs you</dt>
              <dd>{a.cost}</dd>
              <dt>Audio</dt>
              <dd>{a.audio}</dd>
              <dt>Examples</dt>
              <dd>{a.who}</dd>
            </dl>
            <p><a href={a.href}>{a.hrefLabel}</a></p>
          </div>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="where-polyemesis-sits">
    <h2>Where polyemesis sits</h2>
    <p>
      It is the third kind: a relay on hardware you control. What it adds to that
      category is the audio. One SRT ingest carries up to 32 tracks, and each
      destination gets its own stereo mix summed from the tracks you choose,
      server-side, so your encoder uploads once and encodes video once. Video is
      copied through untouched.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is the whole argument, and it is worth being plain about when it does{" "}
      <em>not</em> matter: if one mix suits every platform you send to, a free OBS
      plugin does the job and you should use one.
    </p>
    <ul class="lf-links">
      <li><a href="/how-to-multistream-from-obs">Multistream from OBS, three ways</a></li>
      <li><a href="/docs/audio-routing">How the mix matrix works</a></li>
      <li><a href="/comparison">The full comparison</a></li>
      <li><a href="/free-restream-service">A free restream service you run yourself</a></li>
      <li><a href="/download">Install it</a></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="per-platform">
    <h2>Per platform</h2>
    <div class="rows">
      {platforms.map((p) => (
        <article id={p.slug} class="lf-split">
          <h3>Multistreaming to {p.name}</h3>
          <div>
            <p>{p.body}</p>
            <p class="lf-note">{p.caveat}</p>
            <p><a href={p.href}>{p.hrefLabel}</a></p>
          </div>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>
</InnerPage>
